              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="honest">
      <div class="wrap">
        <h2 class="reveal">The honest part</h2>
        <p class="body reveal">
          Oil is a commodity and its price is set by people in other countries making decisions you can't predict. OPEC production targets, Chinese demand, a recession nobody saw coming — any of these can send crude down 30% in a quarter. The companies above are generating enormous cash today, and that can change by Christmas.
        </p>
        <p class="body reveal">
          LNG Canada Phase 2 is not a done deal. A final investment decision hasn't been taken, and the capital cost is measured in tens of billions of dollars. Phase 1 working doesn't guarantee Phase 2 gets sanctioned on the same terms, or at all.
        </p>
        <p class="body reveal">
          Carbon policy is real and directional. Canada has a federal carbon price, Alberta has its Technology Innovation and Emissions Reduction system, and both add cost that competitors in the Middle East don't carry. Long-duration bets on Canadian oil need to factor in a regulatory environment that is explicitly trying to reduce the product's use.
        </p>
        <p class="body reveal">
          None of that argues for ignoring the sector. It argues for knowing which layer a company sits in, what it has actually signed, and how much of the cash is being returned versus reinvested. That's the job. It's what goes out in the group every morning.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="sec" style="padding-top:44px">
      <div class="wrap">
        <h2 class="reveal">Where nobody's looking</h2>
        <p class="body reveal">
          The top of the ladder is owned by everyone. Every index fund and pension plan in the country holds the big integrated names — Suncor, Imperial, Canadian Natural. Those are priced by people who never read a quarterly filing.
        </p>
        <p class="body reveal">
          Nothing forces anyone to own the bottom two rungs. That's where a company can be supplying equipment to every LNG project on the coast, holding uranium contracts that reset at three times the old price, or sitting on the copper deposit that the energy transition literally cannot proceed without — while the share price waits for somebody to notice.
        </p>

        <figure class="reveal">
          <img
            src={ladder.src}
            width={POR_W}
            height={ladderH}
            alt="A printed ladder diagram with five rungs from equipment and services up to integrated major, with a bracket marking the bottom two rungs that no index has to buy."
            loading="lazy"
